# 7. USER INTERFACE AND DESIGN SYSTEM

## 7.1 Required visual identity

The UI must follow this exact direction:

- Primary background: white.
- Primary accent: light green.
- Text: dark, high-contrast neutral colors.
- Cards: white or subtly tinted surfaces.
- Borders: thin, understated outlines.
- Corners: moderately rounded.
- Shadows: subtle and restrained.
- Typography: readable and consistent.
- Buttons: clearly outlined or visibly defined, with subtle interaction feedback.
- Layout: clean, spacious, and familiar.

Avoid unnecessary gradients, excessive animations, crowded dashboards, decorative charts, and complex navigation.

Do not independently invent a dark theme or change the agreed color palette. A dark theme may be added later as an optional setting.

Use a consistent design system with reusable color, typography, spacing, shape, and button definitions.

## 7.2 Main navigation

Use a simple mobile navigation structure.

Recommended primary destinations:

1. Home.
2. Projects.
3. Settings.

The main creation workspace should remain the primary focus. Avoid adding navigation items for features that are not implemented.

## 7.3 Home screen

Display the Flota name and a concise welcome message.

Main heading:

**"What would you like to build today?"**

Provide a large, accessible message composer.

The composer must support:

- Multiline text input.
- A send button.
- A voice-input button.
- Clear indication of whether a request is being processed.
- Editing the unsent message.
- Recovery of a draft after accidental navigation where practical.

Suggested starter actions:

- Create a Business Website.
- Build an Online Store.
- Create a Portfolio.
- Improve an Existing Website.

These are shortcuts that populate or initiate a request; they must not generate a fake completed website.

## 7.4 Conversation screen

Use a familiar conversational layout.

Display:

- User messages.
- Flota responses.
- Requirement clarification questions.
- Progress updates.
- Tool and test results.
- Warnings and errors.
- Approval requests for sensitive actions.

Support long conversations without unnecessarily re-rendering the entire screen after each update.

Do not make the user scroll through excessive technical logs to understand the current status. Provide an expandable technical-details section for advanced users.

## 7.5 Project workspace

Each project should have a dedicated workspace containing:

- Project name.
- Current status.
- Requirement summary.
- Generated files.
- Version history.
- Preview access.
- Test results.
- Deployment status.
- Export options.

Use clear tabs or sections rather than placing every tool on one screen.

## 7.6 Website completion screen

When a project is complete, show clearly labeled actions:

- Preview Website.
- View Source Code.
- Export Project.
- Edit Website.
- Test Website.
- Deploy Website.

Only show a live URL as active when deployment has succeeded and the resulting URL has been verified.

If deployment has not been configured, explain what remains to be done.

## 7.7 Settings

Include:

- Google account and sign-out.
- Data synchronization preferences.
- Local storage information.
- AI configuration status.
- Privacy information.
- Usage and error details where available.
- App version.
- Data deletion controls.

Never display secret API keys in plain text.

