# 8. CHAT AND VOICE INTERACTION

## 8.1 Text chat

The user can describe a website in ordinary language.

Example:

"I need a modern website for my clothing business. It should have product categories, product details, a shopping cart, contact information, and an admin dashboard."

Flota must identify the requirements and ask only necessary clarification questions.

The user should be able to correct requirements during development without starting the project again.

## 8.2 Voice input

Provide a microphone button for speech-to-text input.

Requirements:

- Request microphone permission only when needed.
- Explain why the permission is required.
- Show a clear recording state.
- Allow recording cancellation.
- Display recognized text for review before sending, unless the user explicitly enables direct voice submission.
- Handle unavailable microphone permissions and speech recognition errors.
- Provide text input as an alternative.

Voice input must not be represented as functional until the actual speech-recognition integration has been configured and tested.

## 8.3 Conversational editing

Support instructions such as:

- "Make the design cleaner."
- "Change the primary color."
- "Add an About Us page."
- "Fix the mobile layout."
- "Add a contact form."
- "Explain why this test failed."

Flota should modify the relevant project instead of unnecessarily rebuilding everything.

Before applying major destructive changes, preserve the previous version and ask for confirmation when appropriate.

