A feature is complete only when:

- Its behavior matches the requirement.
- Its interface is connected to real functionality.
- Inputs are validated.
- Relevant errors are handled.
- Data persistence works where required.
- Permissions are enforced.
- Tests have been run where applicable.
- The result has been reviewed.
- Known limitations are documented.

A visually complete screen is not proof that its underlying feature works.

A successful code-generation response is not proof that the project builds.

A successful build is not proof that the website is deployed.

A returned hosting URL is not proof that the website is accessible.

Verify each stage independently.

